1
00:00:03,878 --> 00:00:05,504
I'm glad to see you made it safely.

2
00:00:05,755 --> 00:00:06,881
How's your hotel?

3
00:00:08,090 --> 00:00:10,258
It's not the best
Best Western I've been to,

4
00:00:10,426 --> 00:00:11,760
but I'd say

5
00:00:12,428 --> 00:00:14,513
it's the third-best
Best Western I've been to.

6
00:00:15,806 --> 00:00:16,973
I know how you feel.

7
00:00:17,141 --> 00:00:20,644
The Best Buy by my house
IS only the third-best Best Buy.

8
00:00:21,938 --> 00:00:24,440
They have the best buys,
but having the best buys

9
00:00:24,607 --> 00:00:27,276
Isn't the only thing
that makes a Best Buy the best Best Buy.

10
00:00:29,987 --> 00:00:32,948
What makes a best Best Buy
the best Best Buy?

11
00:00:33,115 --> 00:00:34,950
Hmm. That's a great question.

12
00:00:35,159 --> 00:00:36,785
I like when they're next to a Chipotle.

13
00:00:39,121 --> 00:00:40,789
Okay, well, I should unpack.

14
00:00:40,957 --> 00:00:43,709
All right.
Enjoy the neurobiology conference.

15
00:00:43,918 --> 00:00:44,919
[ will.

16
00:00:45,086 --> 00:00:47,213
I wish you were here.

17
00:00:48,130 --> 00:00:50,882
At a neurobiology conference?
What a mean thing to say.

18
00:00:53,261 --> 00:00:56,138
Okay, I'm glad you're not here?

19
00:00:56,305 --> 00:00:59,766
Aww. You always know just what to say
after I tell you what to say.

20
00:01:01,644 --> 00:01:02,686
Good-bye, Sheldon.

21
00:01:02,853 --> 00:01:03,979
-Bye.
-(BEEPS)

22
00:01:04,272 --> 00:01:06,023
Oh! Good news, gentlemen.

23
00:01:06,190 --> 00:01:07,983
Amy's at a conference this weekend,

24
00:01:08,150 --> 00:01:10,944
which means I'm available
to be entertained.

25
00:01:11,487 --> 00:01:14,656
As today's youth might put it,
who wants to get their Sheld-on?

26
00:01:17,076 --> 00:01:19,119
Bernie and I are getting the house ready
for the remodel.

27
00:01:19,287 --> 00:01:20,997
We could always use
an extra pair of hands.

28
00:01:21,163 --> 00:01:23,039
That sounds awful. Raj?

29
00:01:23,624 --> 00:01:26,460
Uh, I've got time booked
in the telescope room all weekend

30
00:01:26,627 --> 00:01:27,878
scanning for rogue planets.

31
00:01:28,045 --> 00:01:29,087
You're more than welcome to join me.

32
00:01:29,255 --> 00:01:30,673
That's the one to beat. Leonard?

33
00:01:31,257 --> 00:01:33,884
Oh. If anything,
I'm trying to get my Sheld-off.

34
00:01:34,719 --> 00:01:37,096
Well, then, it looks like
we have a winner. Congratulations.

35
00:01:37,597 --> 00:01:40,266
Well, I should warn you,
it's just looking at data

36
00:01:40,433 --> 00:01:42,101
for hours and hours on a computer screen.

37
00:01:42,268 --> 00:01:44,144
Stop selling it, kid. You won.

38
00:01:45,646 --> 00:01:47,230
(THEME SONG PLAYING)

39
00:02:12,381 --> 00:02:13,715
-Hey.
-Hello.

40
00:02:13,883 --> 00:02:14,758
Hi. How was your day?

41
00:02:14,925 --> 00:02:17,802
Uh, not good.
Still couldn't get in to see Dr. Gallo.

42
00:02:18,346 --> 00:02:19,597
A doctor? (GRUNTS)

43
00:02:21,390 --> 00:02:22,766
Well, I hope you're not contagious.

44
00:02:22,933 --> 00:02:24,392
I've got a weekend in the telescope room

45
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:26,478
I've been excited about
for almost three minutes.

46
00:02:28,731 --> 00:02:29,940
I'm not sick. It's for work.

47
00:02:30,107 --> 00:02:32,567
There's this doctor
who refuses to see any sales reps.

48
00:02:32,735 --> 00:02:33,944
I've been trying to get in for months.

49
00:02:34,111 --> 00:02:37,072
Well, did you try wearing the shirt
I said was inappropriate for work?

50
00:02:37,365 --> 00:02:39,492
Well, the doctor's a woman,

51
00:02:40,242 --> 00:02:41,868
but yes, because you never know.

52
00:02:43,329 --> 00:02:44,371
What kind of doctor is she?

53
00:02:44,538 --> 00:02:46,331
Um. A psychiatrist. Why?

54
00:02:46,582 --> 00:02:48,875
Well, what if you make
an appointment as a patient?

55
00:02:49,043 --> 00:02:50,294
Then you'll get to talk to her.

56
00:02:50,628 --> 00:02:52,755
Yeah, they already know
I'm a pharmaceutical sales rep.

57
00:02:52,922 --> 00:02:56,383
Oh, what if Leonard made an appointment
and tried to lay some groundwork for you?

58
00:02:56,926 --> 00:02:57,968
That's interesting.

59
00:02:58,427 --> 00:03:00,971
Well, I'm not gonna make
a fake appointment with a psychiatrist.

60
00:03:01,138 --> 00:03:02,389
What would I say is wrong with me?

61
00:03:02,556 --> 00:03:03,765
-Low self-esteem.
-Social anxiety.

62
00:03:03,933 --> 00:03:04,975
Sexual insecurity.

63
00:03:07,645 --> 00:03:08,771
None of that is true.

64
00:03:09,105 --> 00:03:11,107
Denial. See, sweetie,
the list goes on and on.

65
00:03:12,316 --> 00:03:13,358
This is crazy.

66
00:03:13,526 --> 00:03:15,027
How would my going in there even help you?

67
00:03:15,194 --> 00:03:17,404
I don't know. You could talk to her,
and maybe if it comes up,

68
00:03:17,571 --> 00:03:18,697
you could ask if she's heard
about the drug.

69
00:03:18,864 --> 00:03:20,031
(SCOFFS) What if I get caught?

70
00:03:20,199 --> 00:03:21,450
-Fear of failure!
-Lack of confidence!

71
00:03:21,617 --> 00:03:22,659
Kind of a wuss!

72
00:03:25,204 --> 00:03:26,288
Fine, I'll do it.

73
00:03:26,455 --> 00:03:28,206
But not because of them,
because I love you.

74
00:03:28,416 --> 00:03:29,417
Thank you.

75
00:03:29,583 --> 00:03:30,584
-Pushover.
-Spineless.

76
00:03:30,751 --> 00:03:33,044
Still hasn't bought milk
even though I told him two days ago!

77
00:03:36,549 --> 00:03:37,550
(EXHALES)

78
00:03:38,509 --> 00:03:40,302
-That was incredible.
-(CHUCKLES)

79
00:03:40,803 --> 00:03:41,887
It was.

80
00:03:42,763 --> 00:03:46,057
I just wish Stuart wasn't around
so we didn't have to be so quiet.

81
00:03:46,225 --> 00:03:47,434
I know.

82
00:03:47,601 --> 00:03:49,060
It's not like he returns the favor

83
00:03:49,228 --> 00:03:52,314
when he watches
his Japanese porn cartoons.

84
00:03:52,481 --> 00:03:54,649
-(CHUCKLES)
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

85
00:03:54,817 --> 00:03:57,194
STUART: You guys got a minute,
or are you still cuddling?

86
00:03:57,361 --> 00:03:58,737
(MOUTHING) Oh, my God!

87
00:03:59,155 --> 00:04:00,656
What is it, Stuart?

88
00:04:00,823 --> 00:04:03,158
-Can I come in?
-Hang on.

89
00:04:04,285 --> 00:04:05,411
Should I send him away?

90
00:04:05,578 --> 00:04:06,662
No, it's okay.

91
00:04:06,829 --> 00:04:07,871
STUART: Thanks, Bernie.

92
00:04:11,667 --> 00:04:12,793
What's up?

93
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:14,378
Uh... Well, I know

94
00:04:14,545 --> 00:04:15,587
the remodel is coming up,

95
00:04:15,755 --> 00:04:19,175
so I thought I'd make it easy
on you guys and find my own place.

96
00:04:20,092 --> 00:04:21,259
Wow. (CHUCKLES)

97
00:04:21,427 --> 00:04:23,429
I thought I was done
getting lucky tonight.

98
00:04:24,889 --> 00:04:26,348
So, when are you thinking of moving?

99
00:04:26,515 --> 00:04:28,308
Uh, actually,
I already found an apartment,

100
00:04:28,476 --> 00:04:29,935
SO in a couple of days.

101
00:04:30,561 --> 00:04:32,396
Oh. Okay.

102
00:04:33,439 --> 00:04:34,857
Sorry to see you go?

103
00:04:36,984 --> 00:04:39,611
Okay, well, thank you guys for everything.

104
00:04:39,779 --> 00:04:41,030
(STAMMERS) I really appreciate it.

105
00:04:41,197 --> 00:04:42,239
You're very welcome.

106
00:04:45,367 --> 00:04:46,368
Hmm.

107
00:04:47,036 --> 00:04:48,037
What?

108
00:04:48,496 --> 00:04:51,373
Hmm, I just have never been in this room
while you're awake.

109
00:04:54,585 --> 00:04:55,586
(DOOR CLOSES)

110
00:04:58,756 --> 00:05:00,174
(KEYBOARD CLACKING)

111
00:05:03,219 --> 00:05:04,261
What are you doing?

112
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:07,973
(SIGHS) Making sure the telescope's camera
is white-field balanced.

113
00:05:08,140 --> 00:05:09,141
Hmm.

114
00:05:10,267 --> 00:05:11,643
What are you doing now?

115
00:05:12,728 --> 00:05:15,605
Still making sure the telescope's camera
is white-field balanced.

116
00:05:15,773 --> 00:05:16,774
Oh, I see.

117
00:05:16,982 --> 00:05:17,983
(SNIFFS)

118
00:05:21,445 --> 00:05:22,571
How about now?

119
00:05:24,323 --> 00:05:27,117
Now I'm making sure the telescope’s camera
is white-field balanced

120
00:05:27,284 --> 00:05:28,785
and wishing you had a coloring book.

121
00:05:30,621 --> 00:05:33,040
Well, I can be helpful.
Give me something to do.

122
00:05:33,207 --> 00:05:34,875
You know, my father took me to work once,

123
00:05:35,042 --> 00:05:36,334
and in ten minutes I figured out

124
00:05:36,502 --> 00:05:38,086
who'd been stealing
from the cash register.

125
00:05:38,254 --> 00:05:39,296
It was my father.

126
00:05:41,131 --> 00:05:42,298
Yeah, Dad lost his job,

127
00:05:42,466 --> 00:05:44,843
but Mr. Hinckley gave me a Fudgsicle.

128
00:05:46,428 --> 00:05:47,929
Fine, you want something to do?

129
00:05:48,347 --> 00:05:50,891
There's about six months of data
on this hard drive.

130
00:05:51,058 --> 00:05:52,768
Why don't you go through it
and see if you can spot

131
00:05:52,935 --> 00:05:54,227
any patterns or anomalies?

132
00:05:54,395 --> 00:05:56,438
Yep, I'm on it. Hey, look at that!

133
00:05:56,605 --> 00:05:59,608
An Indian guy outsourcing a computer job
to a white fella.

134
00:05:59,775 --> 00:06:00,776
(CHUCKLES)

135
00:06:02,403 --> 00:06:05,072
Yeah, okay, whatever it takes
to keep you busy for a few hours.

136
00:06:06,156 --> 00:06:07,240
Found one.

137
00:06:08,158 --> 00:06:09,200
No, you didn't.

138
00:06:09,785 --> 00:06:11,787
There are millions of data points there.

139
00:06:11,954 --> 00:06:14,289
But, look, an optical transient.

140
00:06:15,416 --> 00:06:16,542
Yeah, maybe that is something.

141
00:06:16,709 --> 00:06:18,585
-How did you find that?
-It wasn't difficult.

142
00:06:18,752 --> 00:06:21,546
You know how when you see
prime numbers, they appear red,

143
00:06:21,714 --> 00:06:22,923
but when they're twin primes,

144
00:06:23,090 --> 00:06:25,008
they're pink and smell like gasoline?

145
00:06:27,177 --> 00:06:28,219
No.

146
00:06:28,679 --> 00:06:29,680
Oh.

147
00:06:29,889 --> 00:06:31,181
I guess I'm a special boy.

148
00:06:32,433 --> 00:06:33,976
You know, sometimes when a boy is special,

149
00:06:34,143 --> 00:06:35,394
he gets a Fudgsicle.

150
00:06:36,520 --> 00:06:38,897
Which, by the way,
tastes like the speed of light.

151
00:06:42,568 --> 00:06:44,653
Thank you for seeing me
on such short notice.

152
00:06:44,820 --> 00:06:46,696
Ah! It's my pleasure. I'm curious,

153
00:06:47,489 --> 00:06:49,491
are you related to Dr. Beverly Hofstadter?

154
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:51,993
Uh... She's my mother. You know her?

155
00:06:52,328 --> 00:06:53,329
No, not personally,

156
00:06:53,495 --> 00:06:55,121
but I have read all of her books.

157
00:06:55,497 --> 00:06:57,123
Well, then you know her better than I do.

158
00:06:57,291 --> 00:06:58,542
(CHUCKLES)

159
00:06:58,709 --> 00:06:59,835
Well, I'm not so sure about that.

160
00:07:00,002 --> 00:07:04,339
But I can tell you I do not agree
with her theories on child rearing at all.

161
00:07:05,049 --> 00:07:06,300
Really?

162
00:07:06,884 --> 00:07:09,511
Any chance you find them
cold, cruel, and unsuitable

163
00:07:09,678 --> 00:07:11,805
for innocent little boys
who just want to be happy?

164
00:07:13,182 --> 00:07:14,224
Well, I didn't want to say it...

165
00:07:14,391 --> 00:07:16,184
No, no, say it. Sing it.

166
00:07:16,352 --> 00:07:18,020
Rent a plane, write it in the sky.

167
00:07:20,022 --> 00:07:22,607
Sounds like you're holding on
to quite a bit of anger towards her.

168
00:07:22,775 --> 00:07:25,027
No, I've worked through
a lot of that stuff. I'm better now.

169
00:07:25,194 --> 00:07:26,320
Hmm. Good for you.

170
00:07:26,487 --> 00:07:28,614
Do you know she never let me
celebrate my birthday

171
00:07:28,781 --> 00:07:30,866
because being born
was her achievement, not mine?

172
00:07:32,534 --> 00:07:33,576
That's heartbreaking.

173
00:07:33,744 --> 00:07:37,038
Right? To this day,
I send her a card every year

174
00:07:37,206 --> 00:07:38,290
with a little money in it.

175
00:07:41,710 --> 00:07:44,087
You know, once we get
the house back to ourselves,

176
00:07:44,254 --> 00:07:47,131
we can be romantic in any room we want.

177
00:07:47,591 --> 00:07:50,135
Great. I can finally show you
where the laundry room is.

178
00:07:53,430 --> 00:07:54,431
Boy, who would've thought

179
00:07:54,598 --> 00:07:56,683
when you asked me to move in
and help take care of your mom,

180
00:07:56,850 --> 00:07:58,393
I'd still be here two years later?

181
00:07:58,936 --> 00:08:00,354
-No one.
-Nobody thought that.

182
00:08:02,773 --> 00:08:04,149
Well, that's it.

183
00:08:04,316 --> 00:08:05,608
I guess so.

184
00:08:07,987 --> 00:08:09,154
This is weird.

185
00:08:09,321 --> 00:08:13,821
(CHUCKLES) Yeah. A grown man
moving into his own apartment.

186
00:08:14,034 --> 00:08:15,493
It's crazy times. See ya!

187
00:08:18,122 --> 00:08:19,248
Ignore him.

188
00:08:19,415 --> 00:08:22,709
He's just using humor to express
how happy he is.

189
00:08:24,378 --> 00:08:26,004
It's okay. I know he loves me.

190
00:08:26,714 --> 00:08:27,840
Sure he does.

191
00:08:31,218 --> 00:08:33,178
Well, let us know
when you're all settled in.

192
00:08:33,345 --> 00:08:37,057
I will. And I really can't
thank you guys enough.

193
00:08:37,391 --> 00:08:38,850
-(ENGINE STARTS)
-Our pleasure.

194
00:08:39,476 --> 00:08:41,102
-Bye.
-Good luck!

195
00:08:43,731 --> 00:08:44,773
HOWARD: He's gone!

196
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:46,232
Let's start in the garage!

197
00:08:48,402 --> 00:08:49,403
(CHUCKLES)

198
00:08:56,869 --> 00:08:59,663
Okay, so, once we receive the next image

199
00:08:59,830 --> 00:09:01,915
and compare it to the ones
we've already collected,

200
00:09:02,082 --> 00:09:03,291
we'll know what it is that we found.

201
00:09:03,959 --> 00:09:06,962
Ooh, perhaps
it's a heliosheath scintillation.

202
00:09:07,129 --> 00:09:09,131
Yeah, it could be
a trans-Neptunian object.

203
00:09:09,298 --> 00:09:11,258
(GASPS) Maybe it's a new planet.

204
00:09:11,425 --> 00:09:13,677
Unlikely, but it could be a dwarf planet.

205
00:09:13,844 --> 00:09:16,805
Well, as long as it has a healthy gravity
and all its moons, I'll be happy.

206
00:09:17,931 --> 00:09:21,142
Okay, the final image is coming in.

207
00:09:21,602 --> 00:09:23,145
And the object we discovered is...

208
00:09:23,312 --> 00:09:24,313
Come on,

209
00:09:24,521 --> 00:09:27,232
Daddy needs a livable planet
he can rule with an iron fist.

210
00:09:29,485 --> 00:09:30,777
A medium-sized asteroid.

211
00:09:31,570 --> 00:09:33,697
That's it? How common.

212
00:09:33,989 --> 00:09:36,741
That's the chicken fingers
on the menu of space.

213
00:09:38,035 --> 00:09:39,119
I kind of like chicken fingers.

214
00:09:39,286 --> 00:09:40,954
Yeah, me, too. I was stuck for a metaphor.

215
00:09:41,914 --> 00:09:46,084
Come on, a medium-sized asteroid
is still an interesting discovery.

216
00:09:46,460 --> 00:09:47,502
I suppose it could end up

217
00:09:47,669 --> 00:09:50,672
on a collision course with Earth
and destroy life as we know it.

218
00:09:52,299 --> 00:09:53,717
You dream different than me.

219
00:09:56,386 --> 00:09:58,012
It is kind of cute.

220
00:09:58,180 --> 00:10:00,015
Yeah, it is.
And you know we get to name it.

221
00:10:00,182 --> 00:10:03,101
(EXCLAIMS) We better choose a name
no one can make fun of.

222
00:10:03,268 --> 00:10:06,187
Sir Friedrich William Herschel
didn't do Uranus any favors.

223
00:10:11,151 --> 00:10:13,278
-Hi.
-Hey, how'd it go?

224
00:10:13,445 --> 00:10:16,322
Great. Dr. Gallo is terrific.

225
00:10:16,490 --> 00:10:18,283
You know, I've always been insecure

226
00:10:18,450 --> 00:10:20,702
that no one cares about what I have
to say, but she made me see--

227
00:10:20,869 --> 00:10:22,704
Yeah, no one cares.
Did you help me out or not?

228
00:10:23,497 --> 00:10:25,707
Okay, now, what I'm hearing

229
00:10:25,874 --> 00:10:28,877
Is that you feel that I sometimes
take too long to express myself,

230
00:10:29,044 --> 00:10:30,754
and you wish I'd be more succinct.

231
00:10:30,921 --> 00:10:33,798
You're only hearing that
because I cannot roll my eyes any louder.

232
00:10:34,716 --> 00:10:37,635
All right, well, after we talked about
my issues with my mother,

233
00:10:37,803 --> 00:10:39,805
nothing too deep,
just how she ruined my life,

234
00:10:40,764 --> 00:10:43,516
I told her about you and your drug.
She said she'd be happy to meet you.

235
00:10:43,684 --> 00:10:47,187
Really?
Oh! You're the best husband ever!

236
00:10:47,563 --> 00:10:50,607
So, you'll go to the Doctor Who convention
with me next week?

237
00:10:51,984 --> 00:10:52,943
I guess.

238
00:10:53,110 --> 00:10:55,987
Oh, I actually did hear
your eyes roll that time. (CHUCKLES)

239
00:10:58,365 --> 00:10:59,407
(WHIRRING)

240
00:11:00,784 --> 00:11:03,203
Boy, when was the last time
Stuart cleaned this place?

241
00:11:03,370 --> 00:11:04,371
No kidding.

242
00:11:04,538 --> 00:11:06,122
Oh. Okay, I'm about to suck something up.

243
00:11:06,331 --> 00:11:08,041
What do you think this object sounds like?

244
00:11:08,250 --> 00:11:11,086
Howie, I don't want to play
Lego, Toenail or Pill anymore.

245
00:11:13,505 --> 00:11:15,632
So, what do you think
we should do with this room?

246
00:11:15,799 --> 00:11:18,218
Well, I was thinking
a home theater or a gaming room,

247
00:11:18,385 --> 00:11:20,178
you know, like a man cave.

248
00:11:20,345 --> 00:11:21,721
Why can't it be a woman cave?

249
00:11:21,889 --> 00:11:23,724
As long as it has a home
theater, a video game system

250
00:11:23,891 --> 00:11:24,892
and you're not allowed in it,

251
00:11:25,058 --> 00:11:26,350
you can call it whatever you want.

252
00:11:27,936 --> 00:11:29,228
I'm being serious.

253
00:11:29,479 --> 00:11:31,481
Well, what do you think we should do?

254
00:11:31,648 --> 00:11:34,609
I don't know, maybe a home office or...

255
00:11:34,985 --> 00:11:36,444
Oh... (EXHALES)

256
00:11:36,987 --> 00:11:38,113
Look.

257
00:11:38,697 --> 00:11:41,032
The teddy bear Stuart won
the night we took him to the fair.

258
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:42,576
(CHUCKLES)

259
00:11:43,076 --> 00:11:44,786
He was so excited.

260
00:11:45,412 --> 00:11:46,413
Yeah.

261
00:11:46,622 --> 00:11:48,040
You know, no matter how hard they tried,

262
00:11:48,207 --> 00:11:49,791
they could not guess his age.

263
00:11:51,418 --> 00:11:53,628
I'll give it to him next time I see him.

264
00:11:54,296 --> 00:11:56,339
-Have you heard from him since he left?
-No.

265
00:11:56,632 --> 00:11:59,468
You'd think he would've called
once he got settled in.

266
00:12:00,093 --> 00:12:02,136
-Maybe he's busy.
-Too busy to call?

267
00:12:02,304 --> 00:12:05,640
He wasn't too busy to binge-watch
Hot in Cleveland with my Hulu password.

268
00:12:08,977 --> 00:12:10,103
What is happening?

269
00:12:10,729 --> 00:12:12,480
Are we missing him?

270
00:12:12,940 --> 00:12:15,484
No, that's not what this feeling is.

271
00:12:16,526 --> 00:12:17,693
Is 117

272
00:12:18,737 --> 00:12:20,780
Of course not. He drove us crazy.

273
00:12:20,989 --> 00:12:23,366
Like when you were gonna make that pie

274
00:12:23,533 --> 00:12:26,077
and Stuart ate all the blueberries. God!

275
00:12:26,245 --> 00:12:29,039
And he tried to deny i,
but his teeth were all purple.

276
00:12:31,333 --> 00:12:33,168
-That was pretty cute.
-(CHUCKLES)

277
00:12:33,627 --> 00:12:34,711
Yeah.

278
00:12:35,629 --> 00:12:37,005
Ew, we are missing him!

279
00:12:41,009 --> 00:12:43,386
So I was thinking,
maybe we can come up with a name

280
00:12:43,553 --> 00:12:45,846
for the asteroid by combining our names.

281
00:12:46,014 --> 00:12:47,348
That's a great idea.

282
00:12:47,683 --> 00:12:48,725
I've got it.

283
00:12:48,892 --> 00:12:50,727
We'll call it Cooper.

284
00:12:53,814 --> 00:12:55,273
How Is that both our names?

285
00:12:55,774 --> 00:12:58,526
"Koo" from Koothrappali
and "per" from Cooper.

286
00:12:59,945 --> 00:13:01,863
Yeah, so it's, like, Kooper with a "K"?

287
00:13:02,364 --> 00:13:03,531
Nah, you're right, that's dumb.

288
00:13:05,367 --> 00:13:07,410
-Hey.
-Leonard, great news.

289
00:13:07,577 --> 00:13:10,871
We discovered
a medium-sized asteroid together.

290
00:13:11,039 --> 00:13:12,540
Wow! That's amazing!

291
00:13:12,708 --> 00:13:14,626
I'm in a pretty great mood today myself.

292
00:13:14,793 --> 00:13:16,836
Okay. Guess we're gonna
talk about you now.

293
00:13:17,796 --> 00:13:20,590
Well, we are,
because Dr. Gallo made me realize

294
00:13:20,757 --> 00:13:23,384
that I'm a worthwhile person
and that my feelings matter.

295
00:13:23,719 --> 00:13:26,471
I learned that for free
from a cat poster, but good for you.

296
00:13:29,599 --> 00:13:30,600
Okay, back to me.

297
00:13:30,767 --> 00:13:33,478
I discovered an asteroid,
and now I get to name it.

298
00:13:33,645 --> 00:13:35,104
Wait, what happened to us?

299
00:13:35,355 --> 00:13:36,397
Now, this isn't about us,

300
00:13:36,565 --> 00:13:38,400
this is about what's best
for the asteroid.

301
00:13:40,861 --> 00:13:42,070
What are you thinking of naming it?

302
00:13:42,237 --> 00:13:43,863
I haven't settled on anything yet.

303
00:13:44,031 --> 00:13:45,615
We haven't settled on anything yet.

304
00:13:45,824 --> 00:13:47,450
All right, way to go, Cat Poster.
You hang in there.

305
00:13:50,203 --> 00:13:52,079
You know what'd be nice?
Name it after your girlfriends.

306
00:13:52,247 --> 00:13:53,331
Show them how much you care.

307
00:13:54,875 --> 00:13:56,918
-That is a great idea.
-It's perfect.

308
00:13:57,085 --> 00:13:59,253
It appears romantic, but it's really just

309
00:13:59,421 --> 00:14:02,048
a rock in space that gets me
out of Valentine's Day forever.

310
00:14:04,593 --> 00:14:06,886
So, uh, what were you thinking?
Combine their names?

311
00:14:07,054 --> 00:14:08,180
Mmm. I like It

312
00:14:08,347 --> 00:14:11,266
Yeah, we'll take the "A-M" from Amy

313
00:14:11,433 --> 00:14:12,934
and the "Y" from Emily.

314
00:14:15,437 --> 00:14:16,646
That's just Amy.

315
00:14:16,813 --> 00:14:18,564
Exactly. See how well we work together?

316
00:14:21,443 --> 00:14:22,777
And in double-blind studies,

317
00:14:22,944 --> 00:14:25,071
Placinex proved
extremely effective in treating

318
00:14:25,238 --> 00:14:26,781
all kinds of anxiety.

319
00:14:26,948 --> 00:14:28,991
Actually, funny story, the boys in the lab

320
00:14:29,159 --> 00:14:31,828
were worried about getting
FDA approval for Placinex.

321
00:14:31,995 --> 00:14:33,871
They started taking it, stopped worrying.

322
00:14:34,039 --> 00:14:35,040
So...

323
00:14:35,624 --> 00:14:36,791
Got it.

324
00:14:36,958 --> 00:14:38,125
Do you have any questions?

325
00:14:38,293 --> 00:14:39,544
-Just one.
-Mmm-hmm.

326
00:14:40,670 --> 00:14:42,463
When you made your husband
pretend to be a patient

327
00:14:42,631 --> 00:14:46,635
SO you could get access to me,
what were you thinking there?

328
00:14:48,678 --> 00:14:51,389
I just meant a question about the drug.

329
00:14:53,308 --> 00:14:54,309
Yeah, I know what you meant.

330
00:14:54,476 --> 00:14:58,563
Let's put that aside for a minute
and talk about why you married Leonard.

331
00:14:59,648 --> 00:15:00,815
(SOFTLY) I don't wanna.

332
00:15:03,068 --> 00:15:05,278
Here is a man
raised by an overbearing woman

333
00:15:05,445 --> 00:15:08,823
who completely dominated
every aspect of his formative years.

334
00:15:09,408 --> 00:15:10,700
Do you think

335
00:15:10,867 --> 00:15:15,162
he's perpetuating that relationship
by seeking out a partner like you?

336
00:15:16,915 --> 00:15:20,084
You know, I used to wear tank tops a lot.

337
00:15:20,252 --> 00:15:21,836
That was a big selling point.

338
00:15:25,298 --> 00:15:26,507
Hi, Sheldon. What's up?

339
00:15:27,134 --> 00:15:29,386
Good news. You're an asteroid.

340
00:15:32,597 --> 00:15:35,349
Uh. Please tell me what to say next.

341
00:15:37,018 --> 00:15:38,269
Perhaps I should explain.

342
00:15:38,437 --> 00:15:39,855
While working with Koothrappali,

343
00:15:40,021 --> 00:15:43,315
we discovered an asteroid,
and I named it after you.

344
00:15:43,984 --> 00:15:45,318
Oh. (CHUCKLES)

345
00:15:45,902 --> 00:15:49,447
Sheldon, thank you. That's so romantic.

346
00:15:49,614 --> 00:15:50,990
But what about Rajesh?

347
00:15:51,158 --> 00:15:53,034
He was okay with you choosing the name?

348
00:15:53,243 --> 00:15:55,954
Well, it took a little negotiating,
but I wore him down.

349
00:15:56,121 --> 00:15:57,372
We get the asteroid,

350
00:15:57,539 --> 00:15:59,249
and if you and I have children,

351
00:15:59,416 --> 00:16:01,292
they all have to be named Rajesh.

352
00:16:04,004 --> 00:16:05,046
All of them?

353
00:16:05,213 --> 00:16:06,297
Even the girls.

354
00:16:09,176 --> 00:16:11,052
Okay, I think I know what to say now.

355
00:16:13,805 --> 00:16:15,097
How can I not sound like his mother

356
00:16:15,265 --> 00:16:17,392
when our entire bedroom
is filled with Star Wars toys?

357
00:16:17,559 --> 00:16:20,395
I mean, have you ever had sex
with a stuffed Wookiee watching you?

358
00:16:21,813 --> 00:16:24,357
I went to college in the '70s.
It was a hairier time.

359
00:16:24,524 --> 00:16:25,733
I'm gonna say yes.

360
00:16:27,235 --> 00:16:29,528
You know, If anything,
he's turning me into his mother.

361
00:16:29,696 --> 00:16:31,906
Before I did pharmaceutical sales,
I was an actress.

362
00:16:32,240 --> 00:16:33,616
You know, I was pretty good.

363
00:16:33,783 --> 00:16:36,243
You know, girl-next-door type, but hot.

364
00:16:36,495 --> 00:16:37,621
Doable.

365
00:16:38,538 --> 00:16:39,539
Hmm.

366
00:16:39,748 --> 00:16:41,291
And not only am I Leonard's mother,

367
00:16:41,458 --> 00:16:44,085
but we have this man-child
living with us named Sheldon.

368
00:16:44,503 --> 00:16:47,005
Oh, Leonard talked a lot about him.

369
00:16:47,464 --> 00:16:48,923
I wasn't sure if he was real.

370
00:16:51,092 --> 00:16:52,760
Yeah, he's as real as the fine I get

371
00:16:52,928 --> 00:16:54,679
when I use too much toilet paper.

372
00:16:56,515 --> 00:16:59,434
Wow. You really do have
a lot on your plate.

373
00:16:59,601 --> 00:17:01,185
(SIGHS) I do.

374
00:17:02,354 --> 00:17:04,397
You know, Leonard's right.

375
00:17:04,564 --> 00:17:06,107
Talking to you is really helping.

376
00:17:06,274 --> 00:17:07,775
Oh, I'm glad.

377
00:17:07,943 --> 00:17:11,780
You know, you might also benefit
from a prescription for anxiety.

378
00:17:12,113 --> 00:17:13,614
Okay, if you think it'll help.

379
00:17:13,782 --> 00:17:15,366
Oh! Just don't make it Placinex.

380
00:17:15,534 --> 00:17:17,744
I do not need
sudden fits of homicidal rage.

381
00:17:22,290 --> 00:17:23,457
I miss you.

382
00:17:23,750 --> 00:17:25,126
I miss you as well.

383
00:17:25,544 --> 00:17:27,295
I can't believe I miss Stuart.

384
00:17:28,004 --> 00:17:30,006
I guess I just miss when life was simpler.

385
00:17:30,173 --> 00:17:32,341
ALL: Go, Leonard! Go, Leonard!

386
00:17:32,509 --> 00:17:34,052
Go, Leonard! Go, Leonard!

387
00:17:34,219 --> 00:17:36,387
Remember, girls,
you decide what makes you happy,

388
00:17:36,555 --> 00:17:38,557
not your emotionally withholding mothers.

389
00:17:39,140 --> 00:17:41,267
Go, Leonard! Go, Leonard!

390
00:17:41,434 --> 00:17:44,145
Go, Leonard! Go, Leonard! Go, Leonard!

391
00:17:56,700 --> 00:17:57,826
(INHALES DEEPLY)

392
00:17:57,993 --> 00:17:59,202
(SOFTLY) I really miss this.

393
00:18:28,898 --> 00:18:29,940
English SDH

